        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most riding mowers that won't start have a safety interlock that is not satisfied, a weak or corroded battery, a failing solenoid, or stale fuel. Diagnose by symptom: silence points to interlocks, fuses and the battery; clicking points to the battery, connections and solenoid; cranking without firing points to fuel, spark and air.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Work from the cheapest checks to the most involved, keep the key out and the plug wire off when working near moving parts, and never leave a safety switch bypassed. Then prevent a repeat with fresh, stabilized fuel and a charged battery through the off-season.
          </p>
        </div>

